Theorem 1.2 (Bayes’ Theorem) If \mathrm{Pr}(B) &gt; 0, then 
\mathrm{Pr}(A \mid B) = \frac{\mathrm{Pr}(B \mid A)\mathrm{Pr}(A)}{\mathrm{Pr}(B)}.











NoteA useful partition form



Suppose H_1,\ldots,H_k are mutually exclusive and exhaustive explanations, each with positive probability, and E is observed evidence with \mathrm{Pr}(E)&gt;0. Combining Bayes’ theorem with the law of total probability gives 
\mathrm{Pr}(H_j\mid E)
=\frac{\mathrm{Pr}(E\mid H_j)\mathrm{Pr}(H_j)}
{\sum_{i=1}^k \mathrm{Pr}(E\mid H_i)\mathrm{Pr}(H_i)}.

This form makes the roles clear: updated probability is proportional to compatibility with the evidence times initial probability, with the denominator ensuring that the updated probabilities sum to one.








NoteProof






Proof 1.2. From the definition of conditional probability 1.4,

\mathrm{Pr}(A \mid B) = \frac{\mathrm{Pr}(A \cap B)}{\mathrm{Pr}(B)}.

But the intersection A \cap B can also be written the other way round: 
\mathrm{Pr}(A \cap B) = \mathrm{Pr}(B \cap A).

Using the definition of conditional probability again, 
\mathrm{Pr}(B \cap A) = \mathrm{Pr}(B \mid A)\mathrm{Pr}(A).

Substituting this into the first expression gives 
\mathrm{Pr}(A \mid B) = \frac{\mathrm{Pr}(B \mid A)\mathrm{Pr}(A)}{\mathrm{Pr}(B)}.

This completes the proof.











Example 1.2 (Manufacturing Example) Seventy percent of components come from supplier A and thirty percent come from supplier B. A component from supplier A passes inspection with probability 0.8, while a component from supplier B passes with probability 0.1.

A component is selected at random. What is the probability that it passes inspection?
Given that the component passed inspection, what is the probability that it came from supplier A?


   Solution 
Let

P denote the event that a component passes inspection.
A denote the event that it came from supplier A.
A^c denote the event that it came from supplier B.


Suppliers A and B form a partition. By the law of total probability,


\mathrm{Pr}(P)=\mathrm{Pr}(P\mid A)\mathrm{Pr}(A)+\mathrm{Pr}(P\mid A^c)\mathrm{Pr}(A^c)=0.8\times0.7+0.1\times0.3=0.59.


Using Bayes’ theorem,


\mathrm{Pr}(A\mid P)=\frac{\mathrm{Pr}(P\mid A)\mathrm{Pr}(A)}{\mathrm{Pr}(P)}
=\frac{0.8\times0.7}{0.59}=0.949\ \text{(3 s.f.)}.

1.2 Random Variables
A random variable assigns a numerical value to each outcome in the sample space. For example, when two coins are tossed, X might record the number of heads: the outcomes \{HT,TH\} both give X=1.
Before an experiment, X represents an uncertain quantity. After observing it, we use the corresponding lower-case letter x for its realised value. We use upper-case letters for random variables, e.g. X,Y,X_1,X_2,\dots.

Cumulative distribution function (cdf). For any (real-valued) random variable X,

F_X(x)=\mathrm{Pr}(X\le x).

Discrete case (pmf). If X is discrete, its probability mass function is

p_X(x)=\mathrm{Pr}(X=x),\qquad \sum_x p_X(x)=1.

Continuous case (pdf). If X is continuous, its probability density function satisfies

f_X(x)=\frac{\mathrm{d}F_X(x)}{\mathrm{d}x},\qquad \mathrm{Pr}(a&lt;X\le b)=\int_a^b f_X(x)\,\mathrm{d}x,\qquad \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} f_X(x)\,\mathrm{d}x=1.








ImportantDensity is not probability



For a continuous random variable, f_X(x) is a density height, not \mathrm{Pr}(X=x). A density can be greater than one. Probabilities are areas under the density, and \mathrm{Pr}(X=x)=0 at any single point.









Example 1.3 (Discrete CDF) Let X be the outcome of a fair six-sided die. Find p_X(x) and F_X(3).

   Solution 
p_X(x)=\mathrm{Pr}(X=x)=1/6 for x\in\{1,2,3,4,5,6\}, and 0 otherwise. F_X(3)=\mathrm{Pr}(X\le 3)=\mathrm{Pr}(\{1,2,3\})=3\times(1/6)=1/2.












Example 1.4 Let X\sim \mathrm{Uniform}(0,1). Compute \mathrm{Pr}(0.2&lt;X&lt;0.5).

   Solution 
Here f_X(x)=1 for 0&lt;x&lt;1 and 0 otherwise.

\mathrm{Pr}(0.2&lt;X&lt;0.5)=\int_{0.2}^{0.5} 1\,\mathrm{d}x = 0.3.







1.2.1 Joint, Marginal, and Conditional Distributions
Suppose X and Y have joint PMF p_{X,Y}(x,y) in the discrete case or joint PDF f_{X,Y}(x,y) in the continuous case. From this joint distribution we can define:

Marginal distributions: the distribution of each variable on its own.

Conditional distributions: the distribution of one variable given the other.

Independence: the case where the joint factorises.








Definition 1.6 (Marginal Distributions) If we only care about X, regardless of Y, we sum or integrate out Y: 
\begin{aligned}
p_X(x)&=\sum_y p_{X,Y}(x,y) && \text{(discrete)},\\
f_X(x)&=\int_{-\infty}^{\infty} f_{X,Y}(x,y)\,\mathrm{d}y && \text{(continuous)}.
\end{aligned}
 Similarly, summing or integrating out X gives p_Y(y) or f_Y(y). Note: knowing the marginals does not determine the joint.











Definition 1.7 (Conditional Distributions) For y in the support of Y with positive marginal mass or density, we define 
\begin{aligned}
p_{X\mid Y}(x\mid y)&=\frac{p_{X,Y}(x,y)}{p_Y(y)} && \text{(discrete)},\\
f_{X\mid Y}(x\mid y)&=\frac{f_{X,Y}(x,y)}{f_Y(y)} && \text{(continuous)}.
\end{aligned}
 This describes the distribution of X given Y=y.











Theorem 1.3 (Law of Total Probability) We can recover the marginal by “averaging” the conditional over Y: 
\begin{aligned}
p_X(x)&=\sum_y p_{X\mid Y}(x\mid y)p_Y(y) && \text{(discrete)},\\
f_X(x)&=\int_{-\infty}^{\infty} f_{X\mid Y}(x\mid y)f_Y(y)\,\mathrm{d}y && \text{(continuous)}.
\end{aligned}












Definition 1.8 (Independence) X and Y are independent if and only if 
\begin{aligned}
p_{X,Y}(x,y)&=p_X(x)p_Y(y) && \text{(discrete)},\\
f_{X,Y}(x,y)&=f_X(x)f_Y(y) && \text{(continuous)}.
\end{aligned}
 In this case, the conditional distribution of X given Y is just its marginal distribution: knowing Y does not change the distribution of X.




The following discrete example keeps the marginal distributions fixed while changing the dependence structure. This makes it possible to see exactly what information the joint distribution contains beyond the marginals.


