TipRemark (Notes on Example 4.3)






Remark 4.3 (Notes on Example 4.3). 

Distributional assumptions. No Normality is required: the conclusion holds for i.i.d. observations with finite variance, \mathrm{Var}(X_i)=\sigma^2&lt;\infty.
MLE under Normality. If the data are Normal, the maximum-likelihood estimator is 
\hat\sigma^2_{\text{MLE}}=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n (X_i-\bar X)^2,
 which is biased downward: 
\mathrm{E}\!\left[\hat\sigma^2_{\text{MLE}}\right]=\frac{n-1}{n}\,\sigma^2
\quad\big(\text{bias}=-\sigma^2/n\big).
 See Example 5.12 for full details.












Definition 4.6 (Standard Error) The standard error of an estimator \hat{\theta} of \theta is its standard deviation under repeated sampling:

\mathrm{S.E.}(\hat{\theta}) = \sqrt{\mathrm{Var}(\hat{\theta})}.

It gives a scale for how much we expect the estimator to vary from sample to sample. This is the theoretical standard error, which may depend on unknown parameters. An estimated standard error replaces those unknown quantities by estimates computed from the observed data.






4.2.2 From Error to Mean Squared Error (MSE)
So far we have seen that estimators can have bias and variance. Both are important, but it is often useful to combine them into a single overall measure of accuracy.
To get there, we start with the basic idea of error.







Definition 4.7 (Error) The estimation error is the random difference between the estimator and the true parameter:

\mathrm{Error}(\hat{\theta}) = \hat{\theta} - \theta.





After data are observed this has a realised value, but in practice the true \theta is unknown, so the realised error cannot usually be calculated directly. As a theoretical quantity it captures “how far off” the estimator is. There are two problems with averaging the unsquared error:

The error is a random variable (it depends on the sample we happened to draw).
The error can be positive or negative — so if we just averaged it across many samples, the positives and negatives might cancel out, misleading us into thinking the estimator is more accurate than it really is.

To fix this, we square the error (so it’s always positive) and then take its expectation (so we measure its typical size across many samples).







Definition 4.8 (Mean Squared Error (MSE)) The MSE of an estimator \hat{\theta} of \theta is

\mathrm{MSE}(\hat{\theta})=\mathrm{E}\!\big[(\hat{\theta}-\theta)^2\big].

This measures the average squared distance between the estimator and the true parameter value.










TipRemark (Why Squared Error?)






Remark 4.4 (Why Squared Error?). The MSE is built to solve the two problems with using the error 4.7:

Squaring makes all errors positive (so no cancellation).
Expectation removes randomness and shows the long-run behaviour of the estimator.

But squaring isn’t the only possible choice. For example, we could use absolute error and define the Mean Absolute Error (MAE):

\mathrm{MAE}(\hat{\theta}) = \mathrm{E}\!\big[ \lvert \hat{\theta} - \theta \rvert \big].

This is also a sensible measure. In fact, in practice people sometimes prefer MAE because it is less sensitive to occasional very large errors.
So why is MSE so common in theory?

It penalises large deviations more strongly, which can be useful.
It is mathematically easier to work with. Many nice algebraic decompositions (like the bias-variance decomposition 4.4) only hold neatly for squared error.





Directly using the definition 4.8 of MSE can be awkward. A more useful identity decomposes MSE into bias and variance:







Proposition 4.4 (MSE: Bias–Variance Decomposition) For any estimator \hat{\theta},

\mathrm{MSE}(\hat{\theta}) = \mathrm{Bias}(\hat{\theta})^2+\mathrm{Var}(\hat{\theta}).











NoteProof






Proof 4.4. Start from the definition:

\mathrm{MSE}(\hat{\theta})=\mathrm{E}\!\big[(\hat{\theta}-\theta)^2\big].

Step 1 (add–and–subtract the mean). Let m=\mathrm{E}[\hat{\theta}]. Then

\hat{\theta}-\theta
=\big(\hat{\theta}-m\big)+\big(m-\theta\big).

Step 2 (expand the square).

(\hat{\theta}-\theta)^2
=\big(\hat{\theta}-m\big)^2
+2\big(\hat{\theta}-m\big)\big(m-\theta\big)
+\big(m-\theta\big)^2.

Step 3 (take expectations; use linearity).

\mathrm{E}\!\big[(\hat{\theta}-\theta)^2\big]
=\underbrace{\mathrm{E}\!\big[(\hat{\theta}-m)\!^2\big]}_{\mathrm{Var}(\hat{\theta})}
+2\,(m-\theta)\,\underbrace{\mathrm{E}\!\big[\hat{\theta}-m\big]}_{=\,0}
+\underbrace{(m-\theta)^2}_{\mathrm{Bias}(\hat{\theta})^2}.

The middle term vanishes because \mathrm{E}[\hat{\theta}-m]=\mathrm{E}[\hat{\theta}]-m=0. Hence

\mathrm{MSE}(\hat{\theta})
=\mathrm{Var}(\hat{\theta})+\big(\mathrm{E}[\hat{\theta}]-\theta\big)^2
=\mathrm{Var}(\hat{\theta})+\mathrm{Bias}(\hat{\theta})^2.











TipRemark (Why this helps)






Remark 4.5 (Why this helps). 

If \hat{\theta} is unbiased, then \mathrm{MSE}(\hat{\theta})=\mathrm{Var}(\hat{\theta}).
In general there is a bias–variance trade-off: a small intentional bias can reduce variance enough to lower MSE (common in shrinkage/regularised estimators).





The visualisation below uses a Normal sample and the estimator \hat\mu_c=c\bar X from the next example. Normality lets us draw its sampling distribution exactly; the MSE decomposition itself does not require Normality.


Show Visualisation: MSE and the bias–variance trade-off
Start at c=1, where the estimator is unbiased. Then decrease c slightly and predict whether the reduction in variance will initially be larger or smaller than the increase in squared bias.

