Example 4.4 (MSE of Sample Mean) Suppose X_1,\ldots,X_n are i.i.d. with \mathrm E[X_i]=\mu and \mathrm{Var}(X_i)=\sigma^2&lt;\infty. We wish to estimate the population mean \mu with \hat{\mu} = c\bar{X}, where c is a constant.
Compute the MSE of this estimator.

   Solution 

Bias:

\mathrm{Bias}(\hat{\mu}) = (c-1)\mu.

Variance:

\mathrm{Var}(\hat{\mu}) = c^2 \frac{\sigma^2}{n}.

MSE:

\mathrm{MSE}(\hat{\mu})
= \frac{c^2\sigma^2}{n} + (c-1)^2 \mu^2.


This shows how a biased estimator can still have low MSE if variance is reduced.












Example 4.5 (MSE of Three Estimators) Suppose that a random variable X has expected value \mathrm{E}[X] = 2\theta and variance \mathrm{Var}(X) = \theta^2, where \theta &gt; 0 is an unknown parameter.
Another random variable Y, independent of X, has \mathrm{E}[Y] = 3\theta and \mathrm{Var}(Y) = 2\theta^2.
Consider the following three estimators of \theta:

\hat{\theta}_1 = X/2,

\hat{\theta}_2 = Y/3,

\hat{\theta}_3 = (3X + 2Y)/12.

Compute the MSE of each estimator, and recommend the best one.

   Solution 
We use the rules of expectation and variance for linear functions of random variables (independence of X and Y is needed for the variance of \hat{\theta}_3).

Estimator (i): \hat{\theta}_1 = X/2

Mean:

\mathrm{E}[\hat{\theta}_1] = \frac{\mathrm{E}[X]}{2} = \frac{2\theta}{2} = \theta.

Variance:

\mathrm{Var}(\hat{\theta}_1) = \frac{\mathrm{Var}(X)}{4} = \frac{\theta^2}{4}.

Since unbiased, \mathrm{MSE}(\hat{\theta}_1) = \tfrac{\theta^2}{4}.


Estimator (ii): \hat{\theta}_2 = Y/3

Mean:

\mathrm{E}[\hat{\theta}_2] = \frac{\mathrm{E}[Y]}{3} = \frac{3\theta}{3} = \theta.

Variance:

\mathrm{Var}(\hat{\theta}_2) = \frac{\mathrm{Var}(Y)}{9} = \frac{2\theta^2}{9}.

Since unbiased, \mathrm{MSE}(\hat{\theta}_2) = \tfrac{2\theta^2}{9}.


Estimator (iii): \hat{\theta}_3 = (3X + 2Y)/12

Mean:

\mathrm{E}[\hat{\theta}_3] = \frac{3\,\mathrm{E}[X] + 2\,\mathrm{E}[Y]}{12}
= \frac{3(2\theta) + 2(3\theta)}{12} = \theta.

Variance (using independence):

\mathrm{Var}(\hat{\theta}_3)
= \frac{1}{144}\Big(9\,\mathrm{Var}(X) + 4\,\mathrm{Var}(Y)\Big)
= \frac{1}{144}(9\theta^2 + 8\theta^2)
= \frac{17}{144}\theta^2.

Since unbiased, \mathrm{MSE}(\hat{\theta}_3) = \tfrac{17}{144}\theta^2.


Comparison:
- \mathrm{MSE}(\hat{\theta}_1) = 0.25\theta^2
- \mathrm{MSE}(\hat{\theta}_2) \approx 0.222\theta^2
- \mathrm{MSE}(\hat{\theta}_3) \approx 0.118\theta^2
Thus, \hat{\theta}_3 has the smallest MSE and is the best choice.












Example 4.6 (Recurring example: estimating a Bernoulli probability) Let Y_1,\ldots,Y_n\overset{\mathrm{iid}}{\sim}\mathrm{Bernoulli}(p) and estimate the unknown success probability with 
\hat p=\bar Y=\frac1n\sum_{i=1}^nY_i.
 Since \mathrm E[Y_i]=p and \mathrm{Var}(Y_i)=p(1-p), 
\mathrm E[\hat p]=p,
\qquad
\mathrm{Var}(\hat p)=\frac{p(1-p)}n,
\qquad
\mathrm{MSE}(\hat p)=\frac{p(1-p)}n.
 Thus the sample proportion is unbiased and becomes more precise as n grows. In the next chapter the same rule will reappear as the MLE; in the Bayesian chapter we will compare it with a Beta-posterior mean and MAP estimate.

4.3 Central Limit Theorem (CLT)







Theorem 4.1 (Central Limit Theorem (CLT)) Let X_1, X_2, \ldots be an infinite sequence of independent and identically distributed random variables with finite mean \mu=\mathrm{E}[X_1] and positive finite variance 0&lt;\sigma^2=\mathrm{Var}(X_1)&lt;\infty.
Define the sample mean 
\bar X_n \;=\; \frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n X_i.
 Then, under these stated conditions, 
\frac{\bar X_n - \mu}{\sigma/\sqrt{n}} \;\xrightarrow{\,d\,}\; \mathcal N(0,1)\quad\text{as } n\to\infty.











NoteProof






Proof 4.5. This proof is not examinable.
The proof uses characteristic functions. For a random variable Y, its characteristic function is 
\varphi_Y(t)=\mathrm{E}\!\left[e^{itY}\right],
\qquad t\in\mathbb R,
 where i^2=-1. Every random variable has a characteristic function, even when its moment generating function does not exist.
Step 1: Standardise the observations. Define 
Y_i=\frac{X_i-\mu}{\sigma}.
 Then Y_1,Y_2,\ldots are i.i.d. with 
\mathrm E[Y_i]=0,
\qquad
\mathrm E[Y_i^2]=\mathrm{Var}(Y_i)=1.
 Moreover, the standardised sample mean can be written as 
Z_n
=\frac{\bar X_n-\mu}{\sigma/\sqrt n}
=\frac{1}{\sqrt n}\sum_{i=1}^nY_i.

Step 2: Expand the characteristic function near zero. The Taylor expansion of e^{ix} at x=0 gives 
e^{ix}=1+ix-\frac{x^2}{2}+o(x^2).
 Because \mathrm E[Y_1^2]&lt;\infty, this expansion may be taken inside the expectation.1 Hence, as u\to0, 
\begin{aligned}
\varphi_Y(u)
&=\mathrm E\!\left[e^{iuY_1}\right] \\
&=1+iu\,\mathrm E[Y_1]
  -\frac{u^2}{2}\,\mathrm E[Y_1^2]+o(u^2) \\
&=1-\frac{u^2}{2}+o(u^2).
\end{aligned}

Step 3: Use independence. For any fixed t\in\mathbb R, independence of the Y_i gives 
\begin{aligned}
\varphi_{Z_n}(t)
&=\mathrm E\!\left[
   \exp\!\left(\frac{it}{\sqrt n}\sum_{i=1}^nY_i\right)
  \right] \\
&=\prod_{i=1}^n
  \mathrm E\!\left[e^{itY_i/\sqrt n}\right] \\
&=\left[\varphi_Y\!\left(\frac{t}{\sqrt n}\right)\right]^n.
\end{aligned}
 Applying the expansion from Step 2 with u=t/\sqrt n, 
\varphi_{Z_n}(t)
=\left[1-\frac{t^2}{2n}+o\!\left(\frac1n\right)\right]^n
\longrightarrow e^{-t^2/2}.
 The limit e^{-t^2/2} is the characteristic function of a standard Normal random variable.
Step 4: Convert characteristic-function convergence into distributional convergence. Lévy’s continuity theorem says that if characteristic functions converge pointwise to a characteristic function that is continuous at zero, then the corresponding random variables converge in distribution. Therefore 
Z_n
=\frac{\bar X_n-\mu}{\sigma/\sqrt n}
\xrightarrow{d}\mathcal N(0,1),
 which proves the result.




Note. The notation \xrightarrow{d} means convergence in distribution: the distribution of the random variable on the left approaches the distribution on the right as n grows.
The CLT is remarkable: subject to the conditions in Theorem 4.1—most importantly i.i.d. observations with finite variance—the sample mean becomes approximately Normal as n increases. It does not apply to every possible population distribution.
The following simulation makes the two effects of increasing n visible. For each setting it draws N independent random samples, computes their N sample means, and compares the resulting histogram with the Normal approximation from Theorem 4.1. Try a clearly non-Normal population first.


