Using Definition 4.10 directly to prove an estimator is consistent can be difficult. The following gives a sufficient condition that is usually easier to verify in practice.







Proposition 4.5 (Sufficient Condition for Consistency) An estimator \hat{\theta}_n of \theta is consistent if both the following hold:

\hat{\theta}_n is asymptotically unbiased, i.e.

\mathrm{Bias}(\hat{\theta}_n) \;\to\; 0 \quad \text{as } n\to\infty,

Its variance shrinks to zero:

\mathrm{Var}(\hat{\theta}_n) \;\to\; 0 \quad \text{as } n\to\infty.












NoteProof (Not Examinable)






Proof 4.6 (Not Examinable). Suppose \hat{\theta}_n satisfies conditions (1) and (2).
For any \varepsilon &gt; 0, by Chebyshev’s inequality 1.1:

\Pr\big(|\hat{\theta}_n - \mathrm{E}[\hat{\theta}_n]| \geq \varepsilon \big)
\;\leq\; \frac{\mathrm{Var}(\hat{\theta}_n)}{\varepsilon^2}.

As n \to \infty, the variance term tends to 0, so

\hat{\theta}_n - \mathrm{E}[\hat{\theta}_n] \;\xrightarrow{p}\; 0.

Meanwhile, \mathrm{E}[\hat{\theta}_n] \to \theta by assumption of asymptotic unbiasedness.
Hence,

\hat{\theta}_n \;\xrightarrow{p}\; \theta,

which proves consistency.











Example 4.7 (Consistency of the Sample Mean) Suppose X_1,\dots,X_n are i.i.d. with mean \mu and variance \sigma^2 &lt; \infty.
Show that \bar{X}_n is a consistent estimator of \mu.

   Solution 
Recall that 
\bar{X}_n = \frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n X_i.


Bias:

\mathrm{E}[\bar{X}_n] = \frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n \mathrm{E}[X_i] = \mu.
 Thus \bar{X}_n is unbiased for all n, hence asymptotically unbiased.
Variance:
Since the X_i are independent, 
\mathrm{Var}(\bar{X}_n) = \frac{1}{n^2}\sum_{i=1}^n \mathrm{Var}(X_i)
= \frac{\sigma^2}{n}.
 As n \to \infty, this variance tends to 0.

Therefore, \bar{X}_n is both unbiased and has variance vanishing as n grows.
By Proposition 4.5, it is a consistent estimator of \mu.





In some cases, applying Proposition 4.5 is much harder to do:







Proposition 4.6 (Consistency of the Sample Variance) Suppose X_1,\dots,X_n are i.i.d. with mean \mu and variance \sigma^2&lt;\infty.
Then 
S_n^2 \;=\; \frac{1}{n-1}\sum_{i=1}^n \bigl(X_i-\bar{X}_n\bigr)^2
 is a consistent estimator of \sigma^2.










NoteProof (Not Examinable)






Proof 4.7 (Not Examinable). Let’s first try to use the bias-variance approach of Proposition 4.5.
Bias. From Example 4.3, \mathrm{Bias}(S_n^2)=0 for all n, i.e. \mathrm{E}[S_n^2]=\sigma^2.
Variance. Using Proposition 4.1,

S_n^2 \;=\; \frac{1}{n-1}\Big(\sum_{i=1}^n X_i^{2}\;-\; n\,\bar X_n^{\,2}\Big),

so

\operatorname{Var}(S_n^2)
= \mathrm{E}\big[(S_n^2)^2\big] - \sigma^4
= \frac{1}{(n-1)^2}\Big\{ \mathrm{E}\big[\big(\textstyle\sum X_i^2 - n\bar X_n^{\,2}\big)^2\big] \Big\} - \sigma^4.

Expanding the square gives

\frac{1}{(n-1)^2}\!\left[ \mathrm{E}\!\left(\sum_{i=1}^n X_i^{2}\right)^2
- 2n\,\mathrm{E}\!\left(\bar X_n^{\,2}\sum_{i=1}^n X_i^{2}\right)
+ n^2\,\mathrm{E}(\bar X_n^{\,4}) \right] - \sigma^4
\;=\; \cdots

This leads to non-trivial moment algebra (4th moments and many cross-terms). If one carries it through (assuming the 4th central moment \mu_4=\mathrm{E}[(X-\mu)^4] is finite), the result is

\operatorname{Var}(S_n^2)
\;=\; \frac{1}{n}\left(\mu_4 - \frac{n-3}{\,n-1\,}\sigma^4\right),

so in particular \operatorname{Var}(S_n^2)\le \frac{1}{n}(\mu_4+\sigma^4)\to 0.

A Different Approach.
Using Proposition 4.1 in centred form,

S_n^2 \;=\; \frac{n}{n-1}\left(\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n (X_i-\mu)^2\right)
\;-\; \frac{n}{n-1}\,(\bar X_n-\mu)^2.

By the Law of Large Numbers, \frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n (X_i-\mu)^2 \xrightarrow{p} \mathrm{E}[(X-\mu)^2]=\sigma^2 (finite second moment suffices), and (\bar X_n-\mu)^2 \xrightarrow{p} 0. Since \frac{n}{n-1}\to 1,

S_n^2 \xrightarrow{p} \sigma^2.

Thus S_n^2 is consistent.










TipRemark (Consistency)






Remark 4.6 (Consistency). 

Consistency requires convergence in probability 1.17, not necessarily unbiasedness at every finite n.

In this course, you can always use the sufficient condition (Proposition 4.5) to prove consistency.
Equivalently, if \mathrm{MSE}(\hat\theta_n)\to0, then both squared bias and variance vanish, so the sufficient condition holds.
Unbiasedness alone does not imply consistency: an unbiased estimator whose variance never shrinks need not home in on \theta.











TipChapter checkpoint



For any proposed estimator, ask four questions in order: What is random? Where is its sampling distribution centred? How spread out is it? What happens as n grows? These correspond to the estimator/estimate distinction, bias, standard error/MSE, and consistency.

5.2 Likelihood Function
So far we have described the likelihood informally as “the joint density of the data given the parameter.”
We now give a formal definition of the likelihood function and introduce the notation we will use in the frequentist parts of the course.







Definition 5.1 (Likelihood Function) For fixed observed data \underline{x}, the likelihood function is defined as

L(\theta ; \underline{x}) \coloneqq f(\,\underline{x} \mid \theta).

Here we treat \theta as the variable, and the data \underline{x} as fixed. The domain is the parameter space \Theta (for example, p\in[0,1], \lambda\geq0, or \sigma^2&gt;0).





5.2.1 Illustration (Likelihood with a coin)
Suppose we toss a possibly biased coin twice, where

\Pr(\text{Head})=\theta,
\qquad
\Pr(\text{Tail})=1-\theta.

Let X be the number of heads in two tosses. Then, since this is a Binomial 3.1.2 observation:

f(x\mid\theta)=\Pr(X=x\mid\theta)
=\binom{2}{x}\theta^x(1-\theta)^{2-x},
\qquad x=0,1,2.

There are two interpretations of this:

Probability view (before observing data).
For fixed \theta, this is a probability mass function (PMF) in x. It satisfies 
  f(x=0 \mid \theta) + f(x=1 \mid \theta) + f(x = 2 \mid \theta) = 1.
   It tells us how likely different data values x are under parameter \theta.
Likelihood view (after observing data).
Now suppose we observe x=2. Now, since x=2 is fixed, the same formula becomes 
  L(\theta; x=2) = f(2\mid\theta) = \theta^2.
   This function tells us how plausible different values of \theta are, given the observed data.



