Proposition 5.1 (Fisher Information for i.i.d. Data) Suppose we have an i.i.d. sample \underline{X}^{(n)} = (X_1,\ldots,X_n) drawn from the same distribution with PDF/PMF f(x \mid \theta). Define:

The Fisher information from one observation: 
\mathcal{I}_1(\theta) = -\mathrm{E}\left [\tfrac{\partial^2}{\partial \theta^2}\log f(X\mid \theta)\right]

The Fisher information from the whole sample (i.e. Definition 5.7): 
\mathcal{I}_n(\theta) = -\mathrm{E}_\theta\left [\tfrac{\partial^2}{\partial \theta^2}\ell(\theta;\underline{X}^{(n)})\right]


Then

\mathcal{I}_n(\theta) = n\,\mathcal{I}_1(\theta).











NoteProof






Proof 5.1. For n i.i.d. observations, the log-likelihood is

\ell(\theta;\underline{X}) \;=\; \sum_{i=1}^n \log f(X_i \mid \theta).

Differentiating twice,

\frac{\partial^2}{\partial \theta^2}\ell(\theta;\underline{X}^{(n)})
\;=\; \sum_{i=1}^n \frac{\partial^2}{\partial \theta^2}\log f(X_i \mid \theta).

Now take expectations:

\mathcal{I}_n(\theta)
\;=\; -\,\mathrm{E}\!\left[ \frac{\partial^2}{\partial \theta^2}\ell(\theta;\underline{X}^{(n)}) \right]
\;=\; -\,\mathrm{E}\!\left[\sum_{i=1}^n \frac{\partial^2}{\partial \theta^2}\log f(X_i \mid \theta)\right].

By linearity of expectation,

\mathcal{I}_n(\theta)
\;=\; \sum_{i=1}^n \left( -\,\mathrm{E}\!\left[ \frac{\partial^2}{\partial \theta^2}\log f(X_i \mid \theta)\right]\right).

Since the X_i are IID, each term in the sum is the same:

\mathcal{I}_n(\theta) = n\,\mathcal{I}_1(\theta).











Important 5.4: Computing the Fisher Information of the Whole Sample





In most problems, we work with the Fisher information for the whole sample: 
\mathcal{I}_n(\theta)
= -\,\mathrm{E}\!\left[\frac{\partial^2}{\partial\theta^2}
   \,\ell(\theta;\underline{X})\right].

For i.i.d. data, there are two standard approaches.

Approach 1: Compute \mathcal{I}_n(\theta) directly

Write down the likelihood
For X_1,\ldots,X_n \overset{\text{i.i.d.}}{\sim}\mathrm{Distribution}(\theta), 
L(\theta;\underline{x}) = \prod_{i=1}^n f(x_i\mid\theta).

Compute the log-likelihood

\ell(\theta;\underline{x}) = \log L(\theta;\underline{x}).

Differentiate
Compute the first and second derivatives 
\frac{\partial}{\partial\theta}\ell(\theta;\underline{x}),
\qquad
\frac{\partial^2}{\partial\theta^2}\ell(\theta;\underline{x}).

Take the expectation
Insert the second derivative into 
\mathcal{I}_n(\theta)
= -\,\mathrm{E}\!\left[
   \frac{\partial^2}{\partial\theta^2}
   \ell(\theta;\underline{X})
  \right],
 where the expectation is taken with respect to the model for \underline{X}. You will often use known expressions such as \mathrm{E}[X_i] and \mathrm{Var}(X_i), obtainable from the formula sheet.


Approach 2: Compute \mathcal{I}_1(\theta) and multiply by n
Using Proposition 5.1, compute the Fisher information for one observation and then multiply by n.

Write the PDF/PMF for one observation

f(x\mid\theta).

Logarithm

\log f(x\mid\theta).

Differentiate

\frac{\partial}{\partial\theta}\log f(x\mid\theta),
\qquad
\frac{\partial^2}{\partial\theta^2}\log f(x\mid\theta).

Compute \mathcal{I}_1(\theta)

\mathcal{I}_1(\theta)
= -\,\mathrm{E}\!\left[
   \frac{\partial^2}{\partial\theta^2}
   \log f(X\mid\theta)
  \right].
 Then 
\mathcal{I}_n(\theta) = n\,\mathcal{I}_1(\theta).



Recommendation

If observations are independent but not identically distributed, information is still additive, \mathcal I(\theta)=\sum_i\mathcal I_i(\theta), but the shortcut n\mathcal I_1(\theta) does not apply.
Even in the i.i.d. case, Approach 1 is often just as quick and avoids memorising extra formulas.

For consistency, I therefore recommend always using Approach 1.











Example 5.14 (Fisher Information for the Poisson Model) Suppose we have n i.i.d. observations X_i\sim \mathrm{Poisson}(\alpha). From Example 5.4, we saw the likelihood took the form

L(\alpha; \underline{x}) = \frac{ e^{-\alpha n} \alpha^{n\bar{x}} }{\prod_{i=1}^n x_i!}

Determine the Fisher information 5.1 for:

The whole sample
A single observation


Solution
a. Whole sample
We have

\ell(\alpha; \underline{x}) = -n\alpha + \bigg(\sum_{i=1}^n x_i\bigg)\log \alpha - \sum_{i=1}^n \log x_i!,
\qquad \alpha&gt;0.

Differentiate:

\frac{\partial \ell}{\partial \alpha}
= -n + \frac{\sum_{i=1}^n x_i}{\alpha},
\qquad
\frac{\partial^2 \ell}{\partial \alpha^2}
= -\frac{\sum_{i=1}^n x_i}{\alpha^2}.

Hence the Fisher information is

\mathcal{I}_n(\alpha)
= -\,\mathrm{E}\!\left[\frac{\partial^2 \ell}{\partial \alpha^2}\right]
= -\,\mathrm{E}\!\left[-\frac{\sum_{i=1}^n X_i}{\alpha^2}\right]
= \frac{n\alpha}{\alpha^2}
= \frac{n}{\alpha}.


b. Single observation
For one X\sim\mathrm{Poisson}(\alpha),

\ell_1(\alpha;x)=-\alpha + x\log\alpha - \log x!,
\quad
\frac{\partial^2 \ell_1}{\partial \alpha^2}
= -\frac{x}{\alpha^2}.

Therefore

\mathcal{I}_1(\alpha)
= -\,\mathrm{E}\!\left[\frac{\partial^2 \ell_1}{\partial \alpha^2}\right]
= -\,\mathrm{E}\!\left[-\frac{X}{\alpha^2}\right]
= \frac{\alpha}{\alpha^2}
= \frac{1}{\alpha}.








5.4.2 Cramér–Rao Lower Bound and Efficiency
We can now state one of the most important results in statistical inference: the Cramér–Rao inequality. Under its regularity conditions, it gives a lower benchmark for the variance of an unbiased estimator.







Theorem 5.2 (The Cramér–Rao Inequality and the CRLB) Let X_1,\dots,X_n be i.i.d. random variables with likelihood function L(\theta;\underline{x}), and let \hat{\theta} be an unbiased estimator of \theta.
Then, under regularity conditions,

\mathrm{Var}(\hat{\theta}) \;\geq\; \frac{1}{\mathcal{I}_n(\theta)},
 where \mathcal{I}_n(\theta) is the Fisher information 5.1 for the whole sample.
The quantity \tfrac{1}{\mathcal{I}_n(\theta)} is called the Cramér–Rao Lower Bound (CRLB). It is a lower benchmark for the variance of regular unbiased estimators; it is not guaranteed to be attainable. An estimator is called efficient when it does attain the bound.




To see what “attaining the bound” means, return to X_i\overset{\mathrm{i.i.d.}}{\sim}\mathcal N(\mu,\sigma^2) with known \sigma and consider the family 
T_r=\frac{rX_1+\sum_{i=2}^n X_i}{r+n-1},
\qquad r\geq 0.
 Every T_r is unbiased for \mu. When r=0 the first observation is discarded, when r=1 every observation has equal weight and T_1=\bar X, and when r&gt;1 the first observation is over-weighted.


Show Visualisation: the Cramér–Rao lower bound
Move r away from 1. The estimator remains unbiased, so watch only its spread and variance. Can any unequal weighting move the variance below the bound?

crlbMu = 0
crlbN = crlbControls.n
crlbSigma = crlbControls.sigma
crlbRelativeWeight = crlbControls.relativeWeight
crlbBound = crlbSigma ** 2 / crlbN
crlbEfficientSd = Math.sqrt(crlbBound)
crlbSelectedVariance = crlbSigma ** 2 *
  (crlbRelativeWeight ** 2 + crlbN - 1) /
  (crlbRelativeWeight + crlbN - 1) ** 2
crlbSelectedSd = Math.sqrt(crlbSelectedVariance)
crlbEfficiency = crlbBound / crlbSelectedVariance
crlbAttainsBound = Math.abs(crlbRelativeWeight - 1) &lt; 1e-9

crlbNormalPdf = (x, mean, sd) =&gt;
  Math.exp(-0.5 * ((x - mean) / sd) ** 2) /
  (sd * Math.sqrt(2 * Math.PI))

crlbDensityHalfWidth = 4.25 * Math.max(crlbEfficientSd, crlbSelectedSd)
crlbDensityDomain = [crlbMu - crlbDensityHalfWidth, crlbMu + crlbDensityHalfWidth]

crlbDensityData = d3.range(401).map(index =&gt; {
  const x = crlbDensityDomain[0] + index *
    (crlbDensityDomain[1] - crlbDensityDomain[0]) / 400;
  return {
    x,
    efficientDensity: crlbNormalPdf(x, crlbMu, crlbEfficientSd),
    selectedDensity: crlbNormalPdf(x, crlbMu, crlbSelectedSd)
  };
})

crlbDensityYMax = d3.max(crlbDensityData, point =&gt;
  Math.max(point.efficientDensity, point.selectedDensity)
)

crlbVarianceCurve = d3.range(241).map(index =&gt; {
  const relativeWeight = 20 * index / 240;
  const variance = crlbSigma ** 2 *
    (relativeWeight ** 2 + crlbN - 1) /
    (relativeWeight + crlbN - 1) ** 2;
  return {relativeWeight, variance, bound: crlbBound};
})

crlbVarianceYMax = d3.max(crlbVarianceCurve, point =&gt; point.variance)

























































































































